          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    Everything above, wrapped around an instruction set so that a program rather than a circuit is the thing being proved. A zkVM decomposes into three layers — the ISA, the VM architecture, and the proving layer — and most design disagreements are about where to put work between them.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    This is the object the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0045/" title="What a Verified zkVM Would Mean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0045/" display-uri="dhsorens-0045" type="local">verified zkEVM effort</fr:link> is ultimately aimed at. A machine semantics in Lean is the first half of the statement one wants to prove about a zkVM; <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0027/" title="LC3 in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0027/" display-uri="dhsorens-0027" type="local">LC3 in Lean</fr:link> is that half at small scale.
</html:p>
