                <fr:title text="Instruction Sets and VM Architecture">Instruction Sets and VM Architecture</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Whether to prove an existing ISA or design one for provability. RISC-V won the general-purpose end by bringing a compiler toolchain with it; the custom VMs argue the constraint cost is worth the loss.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>RISC-V (RV32I and extensions) as a target: what makes each instruction class expensive to constrain.</html:li>
        <html:li>Register machines versus stack machines (Miden, zkWasm), and what the memory argument looks like in each.</html:li>
        <html:li>Purpose-built ISAs — Cairo, Valida — and the specific constraint-cost arguments for them.</html:li>
        <html:li>Instruction decomposition and the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002W/" title="Lookup Arguments" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002W/" display-uri="dhsorens-002W" type="local">lookup</fr:link>-based approach to semantics that Jolt takes.</html:li>
        <html:li>The formal ISA specification: what the Sail-to-Lean route gives, and what it does not.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
