                <fr:title text="Trace Layout, Chips, and Tables">Trace Layout, Chips, and Tables</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    How an execution becomes a set of tables. In practice this is where a zkVM's engineering lives, and the details are what <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Y/" title="Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Y/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Y" type="local">Clean</fr:link> has to be able to express.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Chips as separate <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002U/" title="AIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002U/" display-uri="dhsorens-002U" type="local">AIRs</fr:link>, one per operation class, connected by cross-table lookups.</html:li>
        <html:li>The main trace against auxiliary and preprocessed columns; what each costs to commit.</html:li>
        <html:li>Padding to a power of two, and the constraints that must hold on padded rows.</html:li>
        <html:li>Witness generation as a first-class cost, and whether the generator is verified against the constraints.</html:li>
        <html:li>How a single instruction's semantics is spread across chips, and how the pieces are tied back together.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
