                <fr:title text="Precompiles">Precompiles</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Hand-written circuits for hot operations — <fr:link href="/dhsorens-004F/" title="Keccak and Ethereum" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-004F/" display-uri="dhsorens-004F" type="local">hashes</fr:link>, elliptic-curve arithmetic, big-integer work — invoked from the VM instead of being proved instruction by instruction. Large speedups, and a large increase in the amount of hand-written constrained code that has to be correct.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The mechanism: how a precompile attaches to the trace and how its inputs and outputs are tied to VM state.</html:li>
        <html:li>The speedups claimed, and on which workloads they decide the benchmark.</html:li>
        <html:li>The soundness surface: an under-constrained precompile is a complete break, with no compiler to catch it.</html:li>
        <html:li>Extensibility as a design goal, and what a user-supplied precompile has to prove.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why this is the most valuable place in the stack to point <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Y/" title="Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Y/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Y" type="local">Clean</fr:link> at.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
