                <fr:title text="The zkVMs in the Field">The zkVMs in the Field</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    One entry per system: backend, arithmetisation, field, memory model, and the choice that distinguishes it. The point is a comparison table, not a survey.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>SP1 — RISC-V on Plonky3, precompile-heavy.</html:li>
        <html:li>Jolt — <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002W/" title="Lookup Arguments" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002W/" display-uri="dhsorens-002W" type="local">Lasso</fr:link>-based, lookups for instruction semantics, notably small proofs.</html:li>
        <html:li>RISC Zero — RISC-V, read-write and paged Merkle memory.</html:li>
        <html:li>OpenVM — modular and extensible by construction; what its extension interface requires.</html:li>
        <html:li>Stwo and Cairo — circle STARKs over Mersenne31, with a purpose-built ISA.</html:li>
        <html:li>Miden and Valida — stack machine and provability-first ISA respectively.</html:li>
        <html:li>For each: what its soundness rests on that the others' does not.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/525" type="external">SoK: Understanding zkVM</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1" type="external">sp1</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/a16z/jolt" type="external">jolt</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/risc0/risc0" type="external">risc0</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/openvm-org/openvm" type="external">openvm</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/rkdud007/awesome-zkvm" type="external">awesome-zkvm</fr:link>.
</html:p>
