            <fr:title text="A Reading Path">A Reading Path</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    An order to read in, so that filling the corpus out does not turn into breadth-first browsing. Systematisations first, because they fix the vocabulary and the attributions; then the primary sources; then the code.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    In order:
    <html:ul><html:li>Thaler, <html:em>Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge</html:em> — the definitional chain and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Q/" title="The Sumcheck Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Q/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Q" type="local">sumcheck</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367" type="external">SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments and Low-Degree Tests</fr:link> — the whole <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0036/" title="Low-Degree Testing, from FRI to WHIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0036/" display-uri="dhsorens-0036" type="local">FRI-to-WHIR</fr:link> lineage in one place, with the soundness regimes kept straight.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1371" type="external">The Small-Field Turn in Succinct Proofs</fr:link> — <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002K/" title="Finite Fields and the Small-Field Turn" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002K/" display-uri="dhsorens-002K" type="local">field choice</fr:link> as the organising story of recent performance work.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/525" type="external">SoK: Understanding zkVM</fr:link> — the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003R/" title="The zkVM Stack" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003R/" display-uri="dhsorens-003R" type="local">layer decomposition</fr:link> and a comparison across real systems.</html:li>
        <html:li>Primary sources, in lineage order: FRI, DEEP-FRI, STIR, WHIR; then Basefold and Binius.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118" type="external">How to Prove False Statements</fr:link> — read after the soundness notions, not before.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link>, starting from <html:code>OracleReduction/Basic.lean</html:code> and the sumcheck development.</html:li>
        <html:li><fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/clean/" type="external">clean</fr:link> and a Plonky3 AIR side by side — the same circuit in both.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    A page in this corpus is done when its main theorem can be stated with its assumptions, and what breaks when one of them is dropped. Everything else is note-taking.
</html:p>
